  "result": {
    "summary": "Verified _risk_report's level rules (high/medium/low), the wrapped testing-gate in _testing_findings (existing block-severity/script blocks preserved, new risk-level/scan-blocked reasons added, override: and human-reviewed: both bypass and are recorded), rollback's reuse of the original quarantined\u2192testing reason (re-read live from history, so a later-edited reason or a wiped history correctly re-blocks), the findings.json-only persistence (no new file, risk.scan copied by reference from the existing scan dict), and the CLI list '-'/level plumbing. All 7 acceptance tests exist as unittest.TestCase methods with correct imports and match the described scenarios by manual trace. Only issue found is a minor cosmetic regex over-match in the filesystem field, not acceptance-blocking.",
    "verdict": "approve",
    "comments": [
      {
        "path": "orchestrator/skill_discovery.py",
        "line": 300,
        "issue": "The filesystem-path regex `(?<![\\w.])(?:~(?:/[^\\s'\\\"`]+)?|/(?:[^\\s'\\\"`]+))` matches the path segment of any bare http(s) URL too (e.g. `https://service.invalid/docs` yields `//service.invalid/docs` in `filesystem`), since the char before the URL's `//` is `:`, which passes the negative lookbehind. This pollutes the persisted `risk.filesystem` list with non-path entries. It does not affect `level` (the destructive check re-matches for `rm -r`/`chmod` text, which URLs won't contain) and no acceptance test inspects `filesystem` contents, so it doesn't block this packet, but it's worth a follow-up to exclude `scheme://` prefixes.",
        "severity": "low"
      }
    ],
